Wildcards
description: any string key, any position - and what a plain index never sees
- >
  `?` as a segment matches **every string-keyed entry** of the current node; `-` matches **every
  position** - all entries, keyed or not, *including* entries created by bookmarks (the same
  keyless segment that appends in a bookmark fans out in a query). `?` is the keyed subset, and a
  query may open with a wildcard. (The former bracket spelling `[?]` was removed.)
- !!<format: text/plain> |
  team: ?              → :team:alice, :team:bob
  pets: ?              → ∅            (pets has only keyless entries)
  pets: -              → :pets:0, :pets:1
  pets: -: name        → :pets:0:name, :pets:1:name
  : ontos: whole: -     → :thirty      (an &...: - membership)
- >
  A bare position stays a pointer step: it addresses the container's own entry n and **never** a
  bookmark-created member - no position claims - so `: ontos: whole: 0` is empty.
